%T A328034 20,23,26,29,32,35,38,41,44,47,50,53,56,59,62,1,4,7,10,13,16,19,22,25,
%U A328034 28,31,34,37,40,43,46,49,52,55,58,61,64,67,70,73,76,79,82,85,88,91,94,97,100,103,106,109
%N A328034 a(n) = 3n minus the largest power of 2 not exceeding 3n.
%C A328034 For odd n, a(n) is odd. For even n, a(n) = 2*a(n/2).
%C A328034 If n = 2^t*u with u odd, iterations of a() reach the 1-cycle (2^t) when u == 1 or 3 (mod 8), and the 2-cycle (5*2^t, 7*2^t) when u == 5 or 7 (mod 8). Proof: Along an orbit, floor(log_2(x)) never increases, so eventually the orbit stays in some interval [2^k, 2^(k+1)). Then y = x/2^k - 1 evolves by the ternary shift without entering the middle third, hence belongs to the Cantor set. Wall's theorem gives y in {0, 1/4, 3/4}, yielding precisely the stated cycles. Scaling and a(x) == 3*x (mod 8) for odd x > 1 give the classification by the odd part modulo 8. - _Max Alekseyev_, Nov 06 2026
%H A328034 Max Alekseyev, <a href="https://mathoverflow.net/q/345666">Proof that representation 3^n s - \sum_{k=0}^{n-1} 3^{n-k-1} 2^{a_k} = 2^m exists for any s</a>. MathOverflow, 2019.
%H A328034 Charles R. Wall, <a href="https://www.fq.math.ca/Scanned/21-3/advanced21-3.pdf">Rational Thirds (solution to Problem H-339)</a>, Fibonacci Quarterly 21:3 (1983), p. 239.
%F A328034 a(n) = A053645(3*n).
%o A328034 (PARI) { A328034(n) = 3*n - 1<<(log(3*n+.5)\log(2)); }
%K A328034 nonn,changed
%O A328034 1,2
%A A328034 _Max Alekseyev_, Nov 10 2019
